body: "## Summary\nWith `--all-release-tags --release-version-from-package`, the heading for the newest release is unstable across the tagging boundary:\n\n- **Before the tag exists**: `resolvePendingReleaseTag` creates a pending window named from the (unpadded) package version → heading `2026.6.13`.\n- **After the (zero-padded) tag `v2026.06.13` is pushed**: the pending window is suppressed (`releaseTagCandidates` matches the padded variant), and the window heading comes from `formatTagVersion(tag.name)` → `2026.06.13`.\n\nThe committed CHANGELOG (generated pre-tag) then mismatches every later regeneration → `--check` fails.\n\n## Real-world impact\n- pm-web Daily Release run 27447490522 failed at `npm publish`: `prepublishOnly` → `release:check` → `changelog:check` ran post-tag and hit exactly this. (Mitigated in pm-web by publishing with `--ignore-scripts`, like all sibling repos.)\n- Every released repo now fails `changelog:check` locally at HEAD, e.g. pm-beads main today: regen flips `## 2026.6.12` → `## 2026.06.12`. PR-time checks per the standard recipe will rewrite the previous release heading as churn.\n\n## Repro\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/unbraind/pm-beads && cd pm-beads && npm i\nnpm run changelog:check   # FAIL: wants ## 2026.06.12 where file has ## 2026.6.12\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\nNormalize calendar-version tags in `formatTagVersion` (strip zero padding: `2026.06.13` → `2026.6.13`) so headings are stable regardless of tag padding — the candidates logic already treats the two forms as the same release; headings should too. One-time churn: historical padded headings (e.g. `2026.06.09`) would re-render unpadded on next regen in each repo.\n\n## Related observation (same neighborhood, found as abandoned experiment debris)\nBoundary timestamps from git tags carry second precision while pm item timestamps carry milliseconds; a strict comparison can push an item closed at `12:34:56.789` outside a window ending `12:34:56` → item resurfaces under Unreleased. Worth fixing together (inclusive-second boundary semantics: inclusive `until` covers the whole boundary second, exclusive `since` excludes it).\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-06-12T23:02:44Z)\n>\n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-changelog#13 - Use release tag timestamp for generated heading dates [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-changelog#25 - Deduplicate label section tags [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-changelog#30 - Fix breaking-token detection, body-preview body loading, and visible-section semver (`#26`, `#27`, `#28`) [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-changelog#40 - Add release:notes script for deterministic CI notes [merged]\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\": \"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!"
